azgor schrieb:
> 
> This is a collection of YANIs by users of the #nethack IRC channel on
> freenode.net. Please excuse any old ideas that may be present.
> 
> [1]: Shouldn't healers have generally better starting dexterity?
> They've got the knife, which seems to be something of a scalpel,

Erm, they've got an honest-to-goodness scalpel, not a knife.

Anyway, where'd you take the extra dexterity points from? NetHack has
a basic "amount" of stat points which get distributed according to
stat "weights" - every point of extra dex has to come from one less
point in another stat.

> so how
> could they perform operations with 8 dexterity. 

Typical ancient-and-medieval operations would be something like lance
a boil, induce bleeding, hack an arm off. 

> [2]: Is it possible to bury a wand of digging? If so, it should refuse
> to allow itself to be buried, unless it has been cancelled.

Wands of digging aren't intelligent artefacts.

> [3]: Decorative objects.

Those objects are quite likely already there, the game just doesn't
bother managing their whereabouts, because they're - ta-daa! - useless
to the player.

> [4]: Djinnis should cross their arms, blink, and nod as they grant your
> wish as per the TV show. --azgor

Pah. Only when hallucinating.

> [5]: An uncursed ring of levitation hits the ceiling, then falls back
> on top of your head. <-- that ring should hover for a moment, or
> something --BenUrban

Only worth implementing if this is intended as a roundabout way of
identification. How about boots of levitation, then?

> [6]: Vomiting should wipe out any text written in the dust. --muumiponi

No thanks. Next thing someone'll suggest vomiting should leave a
puddle of vomit, which you can pick up and carry around (and drop on
an altar for instant divine anger).

> [7]: Ethereal monsters, such as barrow wights, shouldn't be able to get
> caught in bear traps. --TheShrewdDude

I assume that if they can get caught in bear traps, the game _doesn't_
consider them ethereal.

> [10]: YAFM about a vibrating chair when #sitting on the VS.
> --Chilliwack

Only when sitting on something that can usefully be sat upon - a
chest, box, or (theoretically) throne.

> [11]: To reward uber-fast ascensions, how about something like 10
> points for every turn under 20000 turns? (adjust numbers to make them
> come out right) --Yui

Possible consideration for those who fiddle with a revised scoring
system. Grafted upon the current score algorithm, no.

> [12]: Monks should be able to, like, kick boulders, and with enough
> strength (say 18 or more), break them (it might take a try or two)....
> Who's never seen a monk chop a block of cement in half!?

Me.
A block of cement isn't the same thing as a boulder, anyway. For
simple game balance, i'd say Monks should use a pick like (most)
everyone else.

> [13]: When things "wear" a shield, it says they put it on... don't you,
> like, hold a shield, or strap it to your arm? Maybe a message more
> appropriate would be appropriate.

Oh, an appropriate message would of necessity be appropriate, else it
wouldn't be an appropriate message, ne?

Well, any suggestions?

> [14]: At game end, also report the number of times the player prayed
> (to each god). <and how often you [successfully?] #offered something>

[Collated with [15], which treats a different aspect of the same
conduct aspect] 
I don't quite see the need (the game keeps track of but doesn't
normally tell you how often you read/wrote) - but as another
disclosure option, why not?

> [16]: "You disturb the honorable dead! The grave seems unused.
> Strange...." This doesn't disturb the dead. It's just digging
> downwards. Why would it hurt alignment?

It's digging into a Kenotaph ("empty" grave, to memorise a dead whose
body has gone missing or is buried elsewhere), which still is
vandalising a gravesite and disrespect to the dead.

> [17]: it should be some kind of criminal offence (blow to alignment,
> luck; gain aggravate monster\?) to eat a co-aligned unicorn.. not just
> sacrifice one. --Yui

No. Unicorns are primarily of religious importance. The taboos on diet
are more about social customs; and outside of religion, a unicorn is
just a funny antelope.

> [18]: It should be possible to gain some intrinsics by eating various
> articles of clothing

Funny, but no. The properties of magical armour are so much linked to
the equipment slot into which they go that gaining their powers via
"incorporating" doesn't feel logical to me.

> [19]: According to [some url]
> all the stats for the polearms should be revisited, including damage,
> weight, etc. --Yui

So that's what those people think. What's _your_ opinion on the issue?

I think they might bear some looking into and reducing the variety to,
say, half a dozen. They're pretty useful and balanced with regard to
other weapons, but i find the interface a bit awkward; and without
consulting spoilers it's all but impossible to determine which of them
offer a good damage-to-weight ratio.

> [21]: <Tropsy> I always thought wearing a fedora should make you always
> dodge rolling boulder traps, but then make the fedora fall on the
> ground --Yui

Something like that had been suggested in one of the recent lists.
Forcing a drop of the fedora might be a useful balancing feature.

